    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm putting together some notes for a client who just got diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes. Can you explain the difference between high-GI and low-GI foods and how that actually affects their insulin response? Need to keep it pretty straightforward for them.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, I'd be happy to help. High-GI foods are carbohydrates that break down quickly and cause a rapid rise in blood sugar and insulin levels. Low-GI foods, on the other hand, are carbohydrates that take longer to break down, leading to a slower, more stable increase in blood sugar and insulin response. For individuals with Type 2 diabetes, focusing on low-GI foods can help better manage blood sugar levels and improve overall health.",
      "source": "target",
